A persistent `{{ .Shell }}` terminal. `command` is the literal keystrokes: a command line at the prompt (Enter implied); while a program runs, its bytes - keys with no character of their own go in as their terminal sequences via JSON escapes (`\n` or `\r` Enter, `\t` tab, `\u0003` ctrl-c, `\u001b` escape, `\u001b[A` up, `\u001b[3~` delete).

Several shell calls in one step run in parallel, each in its own terminal. Omit `session` and the tool assigns one: a single call (or the first of several) reuses the stable default whose cwd, environment and credentials persist across calls, and the rest each get a fresh terminal. Pin a terminal by `session` name to keep a long-lived program - a dev server, a watcher - running and poll it with empty calls. A line typed while a session's command runs is queued for the next prompt; an answer to a waiting program needs an explicit `\r` or `\n`. A call returns when the process exits (with its exit code), stops for input, takes over the screen (a rendered {{ .DefaultCols }}x{{ .DefaultRows }} frame), or goes idle; an empty call waits for the next of those. A bare `sleep` is refused: waiting on a condition belongs in the command that checks it. A session whose shell has exited reports its final output and exit code once, then reopens fresh in its original directory. `reset` kills the named session and opens a fresh one; last resort.

Pagers are replaced with `cat` and color is off, so output arrives as plain text. A result ends with `<cwd>…</cwd>` only when the shell moved. Output over {{ .MaxOutputLength }} characters keeps a head and a long tail.
